    "message": {
     "content": "This is a **solid baseline offer** for a Google L6 Product Manager, but **you should not accept it as-is**. Because you were passively sourced, are happy at Meta, and carry no risk of walking away, you are in a position of maximum leverage. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Offer Evaluation vs. Current Meta Comp\n\n| Component | Meta (IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Analysis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **Lower by $5,000.** The posted band goes up to $271,000. |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000.** Google\u2019s target PM bonus percentage is slightly higher. |\n| **Equity (Year 1)** | ~$200,000\u2013$250,000 | ~$270,000 (flat) or ~$356,000 (front-loaded) | **Higher.** *Note:* Google typically vests GSUs front-loaded (33% Y1 / 33% Y2 / 22% Y3 / 12% Y4 or 38/32/20/10). |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | **$0** | **Major gap.** Standard L6 offers frequently include $50k\u2013$100k+. |\n| **Total Comp (Y1)** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000 \u2013 $638,000** | Minor bump on paper, but lacks an upfront incentive to offset unvested Meta stock. |\n\n#### Key Takeaways on Google's Pitch:\n* **The \"Refresh Stacking\" Argument:** The recruiter\u2019s explanation about refreshes stacking over 4 years is accurate, but **Meta operates on the exact same model**. Meta\u2019s annual equity refreshes also vest over 4 years and stack similarly. You should treat Google's refresh potential as neutral, not as a premium over Meta.\n* **Front-Loaded Vesting Cliff:** If Google uses their standard front-loaded vesting (33/33/22/12), your year 1 and 2 comp will look high, but year 3 and 4 comp will drop significantly unless large refreshes materialize.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Verdict: Negotiate\n\n**Do not accept yet, and do not walk away without countering.**\n\nA lateral move from Meta IC6 to Google L6 without a promotion or substantial premium is not worth the switching risk unless you get a clear financial upside.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nWhen negotiating, target the top quartile of the Google L6 PM band:\n\n1. **Sign-on Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n   * *Why:* You are walking away from at least $200,000 of unvested Meta equity over the next 12 months. Sign-on bonuses are the easiest bucket for Google recruiters to pull from without needing special equity committee approvals.\n2. **Base Salary: Ask for $255,000**\n   * *Why:* Their offer ($235k) is a pay cut on base salary compared to your current Meta base ($240k). The posted ceiling is $271,000, so $255,000 is well within range.\n3. **Equity Grant (GSUs): Ask for $1,250,000 over 4 years** (up from $1,080,000)\n   * *Why:* Top-of-band L6 PM equity grants at Google can range between $1.2M and $1.35M.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nKeep your tone collaborative, positive, and firm. Emphasize that because you are happy in your current role, the economics need to justify making the jump.\n\n#### Suggested Email / Call Script:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve really enjoyed getting to know the team and the scope of the role is exciting.*\n> \n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum and unvested equity at Meta, making a move needs to make clear financial sense. At Meta, my base is already $240,000 and I have over $200,000 in equity scheduled to vest over the next 12 months, alongside stacking annual refreshes.*\n> \n> *To offset walking away from my current trajectory and make this an immediate 'yes', I\u2019m looking for:*\n> 1. *A base salary of **$255,000** (to avoid taking a step backward on base).*\n> 2. *An equity grant of **$1,250,000** over 4 years.*\n> 3. *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000** to bridge the unvested equity gap.*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign and lock in my start date.\"*\n\n### Next Steps:\n* Clarify with the recruiter whether the equity schedule is **front-loaded (33/33/22/12)** or flat (25% per year), as this will affect your Year 1 cash flow.\n* Stand firm on the **sign-on bonus** and **base bump**\u2014Google almost always leaves room for these two items in their first offer.",
